de with your phone to find a  competitor's pricing share thoughts or  recommendations the app received  positive reviews and was growing  steadily but mobile commerce was an  extremely competitive market back in  2010. it's now extremely common for  startups to spend years completely  focused on building a great product  before trying to make money but back  then questions about profitability  popped up much earlier can you tell me  though how far along you need to be  before you start to make a profit to be  honest we're a ways away from that our  business model is to create a shopping  application that people want to use when  they're in a store to help them make  better decisions about what to buy so in  2012 groupon acquired blake's company  for an undisclosed sum and shut down  barcode hero it doesn't look like it was  an amazing outcome but blake clearly  wasn't deterred he wanted to go bigger  with his next startup so he set his  sights higher literally  blake had been flying planes since he  was in college and always dreamt of  having a real impact on the aviation  industry he didn't have the perfect  resume to start a company that builds  airplanes but that didn't bother him  after all the wright brothers had only  built bicycles before they made the  first airplane and elon musk was running  a payments company before launching  spacex so blake founded boom supersonic  and began building a team around the  idea of safe and sustainable high-speed  travel one of the first major milestones  for the company was joining the startup  accelerator y combinator although yc is  typically known for funding software  startups like airbnb stripe and coinbase  the partners at y combinator love all  ambitious entrepreneurs building  physical things though is usually much  harder than writing software there are  long research and development cycles  expensive manufacturing bills and plenty  of regulatory hurdles but blake  developed a really interesting method  for overcoming these challenges at boom  we believe that life happens in person  and that real face time  beats facetime blake is obsessed with  the idea that in-person interactions are  inherently more valuable than  conversations over the phone or email  and he really lives this mantra why  combinator's demo day was quickly  approaching and blake was going to have  to present boom to a huge group of  investors he needed to demonstrate that  airlines would actually buy the  supersonic planes he was going to build  emails and phone calls to airlines were  going nowhere so he decided to hop on a  plane and get some face time with  richard branson virgin galactic was  rolling out a new spaceship and blake  knew that this was an incredible  opportunity we ended up we ended up  crashing the rollout i never actually  got my name on the invite list but we've  got our 15 minutes with richard branson  we're not asking you to invest in the  company uh we're asking when the  airplane is delivered whether you'd like  to have the first few with a virgin logo  on the tail this was a huge moment for  the company everyone knows richard  branson and virgin airlines so overnight  boom became much more legitimate in the  eyes of investors most importantly  virgin didn't actually have to spend any  money up front so it was easy to get the  deal approved i was actually at the yc  demo day where blake first presented  boom to investors and the reaction was  pretty amazing everyone loved the idea  and couldn't wait to invest none of the  specifications of the airplane had been  decided yet after all the company was  still brand new but blake had a clear  vision for what would be most important  to airlines speed safety and  sustainability these might seem like  vague marketing terms but they are  actually extremely calculated based on  the previous failures of supersonic  development in order to understand why  blake is so focused on these particular  values we have to go back to 1969.  america had just landed a man on the  moon and the first commercial supersonic  airplane was about to take flight it was  the concorde and it had a lot in common  with boom's new aircraft on the concorde  a trip from new york to london took just  over three hours and it felt like the  dawn of an entirely new age throughout  human history increases in the speed of  transport have had remarkable second  order effects in the 1840s crossing the  united states required traveling in a  covered wagon the journey took months  and people often died in transit but in  1869 the transcontinental railroad  shortened that trip to just 83 hours  america went through a remarkable period  of growth as people migrated westward  and in the 1930s transcontinental  airlines began moving americans across  the country in just 20 hours a few  decades later jet engines cut that time  down to just six hours and travel  exploded the jet age affected culture in  remarkable ways the beatles were able to  go on the first world tour and become an  international sensation and major league  baseball became a national sport played  all over the united states not just in  the northeast it's always hard to  predict the effects of faster travel but  throughout history great things always  happen when we start moving faster the  concorde promised to make international  travel dramatically more accessible but  there were still a ton of problems and  most of them came back to speed safety  and sustainability speed was obviously  the main selling point of the concord  business travelers value their time  extremely highly and are willing to pay  lots of money to save time but it's also  just cool to go fast and celebrities  loved how fast the concorde went  it's always exciting flying supersonic  and it's always exciting to get to new  york before you've left but traveling at  mach 2 can cause some real issues the  first of which was heat the concorde  went so fast that the plane would heat  up substantially the entire fuselage had  to be coated in a highly reflective  white paint in order to deflect as much  light as possible one concord was  painted blue during a promotion for  pepsi and wound up only being able to  stay at mach 2 for 20 minutes because of  the heat buildup the concorde was built  before computer simulation allowed for  engineers to test performance in virtual  environments so a lot of these issues  had to be worked out through painstaking  experiments but boom is building their  planes using the latest software tools  the second issue was fuel you would  expect a plane that goes twice as fast  to use twice as much fuel but the  concord actually used more than five  times as much fuel as modern commercial  airliners this meant that tickets had to  be extremely expensive to offset the  cost of fuel and every trip came with  significant carbon emissions boom is  leveraging advances in engine design  that yield greater fuel efficiency which  will mean cheaper ticket prices but they  aren't stopping there boom plans to use  fuel made from carbon that was removed  from the atmosphere meaning that  supersonic travel will be carbon neutral  from day one increased fuel efficiency  won't just make new york to london trips  more affordable though it will actually  open up entirely new routes the concorde  was never able to fly across the pacific  but boom has planned to offer service  from san francisco to tokyo since day  one these new routes will expand the  market for these planes and give boom a  better chance at winning blake also  thinks about pricing differently than  the executives who ran the concord  project he wants to continually bring  prices down until you can get anywhere  in the world for just a hundred bucks  but the concord executives were much  more focused on short-term profitability  british airways and air france actually  performed market research to inform  their pricing strategy they found that  people generally thought of the concord  as extremely expensive so they raised  their prices to match expectations this  is fine when your customer is a rich  business traveler but if you're catering  to wealthy people you need to have a  perfect safety record and safety is what  really did the concord in news tonight  this tuesday the first concord ever to  crash just after takeoff from paris  113 people are killed which included  everyone on board shortly after the turn  of the millennium air france flight 4590  crashed during takeoff and killed  everyone on board the plane had run over  a piece of debris on the runway and  ruptured a tire it destroyed the  concorde's reputation and grounded the  entire fleet for months one year later  the september 11th terrorist attacks  crushed the entire airline industry and  forced the concorde into early  retirement  but boom is learning from all of this  airplane safety protocols have advanced  significantly in the past two decades  and the problem with the concorde's  tires has been thoroughly investigated  many of the safety issues with the  concorde came from the fact that  engineers on the project had a goal of  delivering a supersonic plane at  essentially any cost the original budget  for the concord was 70 million but the  project wound up costing more than a  billion engineers had to make lots of  compromises to get the concorde to work  one of which was the nose of the  airplane because the plane landed at  such a steep angle the pilots couldn't  see the runway as they approached so the  entire nose cone of the plane would tilt  down during landing boom just installed  the camera in the front of the plane  there's like camera toggle button you  can hit and it flips over and you get  all your flight controls overlaid on top  of what is a virtual window through the  nose of the airplane and so you get you  get better runway visibility than you  get with with a traditional subsonic  cockpit it's such a simple solution that  significantly reduces complexity i love  it another issue that plagued the  concord was the fact that it required a  flight engineer to be on board during  every flight essentially the plane was  so complex that a trained expert had to  sit in the cockpit with the pilots just  to ensure everything went smoothly this  created a ton of extra cost and even  with the flight engineer most airlines  still had to have an extra concorde  sitting on standby at all times this is  because concord passengers were so  demanding that no airline could afford  to frustrate customers with delays if  there were maintenance issues boom is  building for reliability from day one  and by simplifying the design of the  plane and using software solutions  wherever possible they should be able to  avoid many of the pitfalls that the  concord faced lastly there is the issue  of noise the fact that blake named his  company boom is pretty funny since the  sonic boom was one of the major issues  of the concord sonic booms are so  powerful that they can break windows in  houses below and for that reason the  united states has put a speed limit on  aircraft traveling over the country but  nasa engineers are working on solving  the issue of the sonic boom as well it's  unlikely that they'll ever be able to  muffle it entirely but there's a good  chance that they could lower the boom  from 105 decibels to just 70 decibels  and at that volume u.s lawmakers might  revisit the speed limit if it means  getting from california to washington dc  in half the time strict regulation is  just one of the many factors that has  led to stagnation in the aerospace  industry and prominent investors like  peter thiel have been calling for  america to address this for years now i  often think it's worth looking at  quantity not quality and we're not going  faster you know the concorde was  decommissioned in 2003 and if you  include low-tech airport security  mechanisms we're back to about 1960 type  travel speeds uh today in the u.s um  part of the problem with the concord was  that ultimately it was a vanity project  by the uk and french governments they  had amazing engineers working on the  technology but they never had a strong  economic model but boom is thinking of  the business model from day one and they  already have signed contracts with  airlines only 14 concords ever saw  commercial service but united airlines  has already committed to buying 20  planes from boom that's really solid  progress but boom does have some strong  competition regular subsonic planes are  getting faster and more efficient it's  also possible to have a more productive  flight on a traditional plane now that  you can use the wi-fi to get some work  done or sleep in a lay flat seat in  first class there are also other  companies working on developing  supersonic planes like spike aerospace  and arion supersonic although arion  recently went out of business but i  think boom's real competition will come  from outside the traditional airline  industry there are several companies  working on planes capable of vertical  takeoff and landing which could change  the way we travel even if everything is  still subsonic a huge amount of any trip  is spent just getting to and from the  airport and these new vtol aircraft will  make it much easier to get to smaller  airports then their spacex point to  point elon musk thinks he can get  passengers from new york to tokyo in  just 30 minutes using a rocket this is  clearly a long shot but if it works it  would be substantially faster than  flying in a supersonic plane lastly we  can't forget about the exponential  growth of the internet every year  internet speeds get faster and more  people get connected video conferencing  just saw a massive surge in adoption  during the pandemic and people are more  comfortable doing business remotely now  virtual reality is still in its infancy  but facebook is working hard to make  communication in vr indistinguishable  from reality this is all part of a  larger initiative to build what people  are calling a metaverse but that's a  topic for another video thanks for  watching
 

